Subject: [PATCH] appworld: stop re-paginating an already-paginated page

The local responder already returns a single page (appworld_wm_prompt.py:313-315),
and the WM prompt asks for one page too. The scheduler then applied the page offset
a second time, so any request past page 0 sliced a list that was already the page
and came back empty while total still reported the full count. With the agent told
to page until the response is empty (appworld_prompt.py:36), a sweep stops early.

Reproduced on appworld_rl_split_clean.jsonl row 692c77d_2: an 80-song library at
page_limit=20 yields page 0 correct and pages 1-3 empty, so the agent sees 20 of 80.
A first-ever request for page_index=1 is empty too, so this is not cache staleness.
Out-of-order access served page 1's records labelled as page 0.

Two related effects go away with it. The old loop took the first non-empty list
field of any dict response, so single-record reads such as spotify__show_playlist
had an unrelated field truncated to the default page_limit and gained a fabricated
total. And page_limit=0 or page_index=-1 disagreed with _page_args' clamps.

The re-page branch is kept for the case it was there for: a world model that
returns more than one page, which is Table 7 (i) in the paper. It now fires only
when the reply is longer than one page, binds the collection key from
RETURN_KEY_BY_SECTION instead of guessing, and reuses _page_args so both layers
agree on the window. _list_caches is dropped; it stored one page as if it were the
whole collection and was never evicted.

Applies to 58e6fe0c963ee4256f6bb02ee99f6b47dc3feb2e
Apply with: git apply -p1 appworld_plugin_pagination.patch

